<tr id="A4.T7.5.1.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A4.T7.5.1.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">DCI (<math id="A4.T7.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math>)</td>
<td id="A4.T7.5.1.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><math id="A4.T7.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.096" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.096</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.096</annotation></semantics></math>0.024</td>
<td id="A4.T7.5.1.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><math id="A4.T7.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.188" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.188</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.188</annotation></semantics></math>0.042</td>
<td id="A4.T7.5.1.7.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><math id="A4.T7.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.253" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.253</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.253</annotation></semantics></math>0.043</td>
<td id="A4.T7.5.1.7.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><math id="A4.T7.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.284" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.284</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.284</annotation></semantics></math>0.060</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2 </span>The relationship between <math id="A4.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> and disentanglement</h3>

<div id="A4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To further investigate the properties of latent representations learned at different <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> values, we plot the evolution of the MIG score as a function of <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> using the Cars3d dataset. These results are presented in <a href="#A4.F4" title="In D.2 The relationship between 𝛽 and disentanglement ‣ Appendix D Additional Results ‣ Denoising Multi-𝛽 VAE: Representation Learning for Disentanglement and Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. The plot reveals that MIG scores fluctuate in alignment with the loss function (<a href="#S3.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ 3.1 Conditional Multi-level 𝛽-VAE ‣ 3 Multi-𝛽 Representation Learning ‣ Denoising Multi-𝛽 VAE: Representation Learning for Disentanglement and Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) associated with each <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> value. For models like <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-TCVAE, <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-VAE, and FactorVAE, determining an optimal coefficient for regularization can be challenging, often requiring numerous training runs as the ideal coefficient depends on both the dataset and model architecture. In our approach, this issue is mitigated by training a conditional <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> model across a wide range of <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> values (<a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Conditional Multi-level 𝛽-VAE ‣ 3 Multi-𝛽 Representation Learning ‣ Denoising Multi-𝛽 VAE: Representation Learning for Disentanglement and Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>).
Building on this analysis, we highlight an additional insight gained from using multiple <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> values. By leveraging the distinct generative factors in the toy datasets, we plotted the highest mutual information for two factors across all <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> values, <span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">showing that while mutual information (MI) increases for ‘Pitch’ as <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> increases, it decreases for ‘Identity’ (see <a href="#A4.F4" title="In D.2 The relationship between 𝛽 and disentanglement ‣ Appendix D Additional Results ‣ Denoising Multi-𝛽 VAE: Representation Learning for Disentanglement and Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>)</span>. This observation supports the idea of training a model across multiple <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> values, as it enables targeted manipulation of image factors by selecting specific <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> values.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2507.06613v1/mig_and_mi.svg" id="A4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:333/134;" width="333" height="134"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A4.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="A4.F4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Variation of MIG and MI:<span id="A4.F4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
We analyze the variation in MIG and MI scores of generative factors across different <math id="A4.F4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> values on the Cars3D dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Reed et al., 2015</a>)</cite> to quantitatively track disentanglement in the latent space. Notably, the highest MIG scores for each factor are achieved at different <math id="A4.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> values.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.3 </span>Multi-<math id="A4.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> VAE Reconstructions</h3>

<div id="A4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#A4.F5" title="In D.3 Multi-𝛽 VAE Reconstructions ‣ Appendix D Additional Results ‣ Denoising Multi-𝛽 VAE: Representation Learning for Disentanglement and Generation" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a><span id="A4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> shows reconstructions generated solely by our multi-</span><math id="A4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A4.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> VAE on the image datasets. To create these images, the same value of </span><math id="A4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A4.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> is passed to both the encoder and decoder networks. The figure displays a range of </span><math id="A4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A4.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> values, demonstrating that the latent representation loses information about the input image as </span><math id="A4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A4.SS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> increases. We would like to emphasize again that our non-linear diffusion model can generate additional information and decode the latent representation into high-fidelity images at any </span><math id="A4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A4.SS3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> values, as shown in </span><a href="#S6.F2" title="In 6.2 Evaluating Generation Quality ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Denoising Multi-𝛽 VAE: Representation Learning for Disentanglement and Generation" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a><span id="A4.SS3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><img src="2507.06613v1/recons_only_bed2.png" id="A4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/192;" width="476" height="192" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><img src="2507.06613v1/recons_only_ffhq3.png" id="A4.F5.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/192;" width="476" height="192" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><img src="2507.06613v1/recons_only_celebahq.png" id="A4.F5.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/192;" width="476" height="192" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A4.F5.21" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="A4.F5.22" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
This figure presents examples of how our multi-<math id="A4.F5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> VAE reconstructs images across different datasets as <math id="A4.F5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> is varied. The top panel shows reconstructions on LSUN-Bedrooms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2015</a>)</cite>, the middle panel displays FFHQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karras et al., 2019</a>)</cite> reconstructions, and the bottom panel contains CelebA-HQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karras et al., 2018</a>)</cite> reconstructions. Please refer to <a href="#S6.F2" title="In 6.2 Evaluating Generation Quality ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Denoising Multi-𝛽 VAE: Representation Learning for Disentanglement and Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> for reconstructed images through multi-<math id="A4.F5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> VAE and non-linear diffusion model.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.4 </span>Generated Samples from Noise</h3>

<div id="A4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">In </span><a href="#A4.F6" title="In D.4 Generated Samples from Noise ‣ Appendix D Additional Results ‣ Denoising Multi-𝛽 VAE: Representation Learning for Disentanglement and Generation" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a><span id="A4.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, we show samples generated by our method, which combines a multi-</span><math id="A4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A4.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> VAE with a non-linear diffusion model, on the image datasets listed in </span><a href="#S6.T3" title="In 6.2 Evaluating Generation Quality ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ Denoising Multi-𝛽 VAE: Representation Learning for Disentanglement and Generation" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a><span id="A4.SS4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. To generate samples, we begin by sampling a batch of noise from a standard normal distribution and then apply </span><a href="#algorithm2" title="In 4.2 Non-linear Diffusion in Latent Space ‣ 4 Reversing the Information Loss ‣ Denoising Multi-𝛽 VAE: Representation Learning for Disentanglement and Generation" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Algorithm</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a><span id="A4.SS4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> to denoise it to </span><math id="A4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A4.SS4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. In the final step, we decode the denoised latent sample into the pixel space using the trained decoder as </span><math id="A4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}=g_{\theta}(\bm{z}_{0},\beta=0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒙</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">g</mi><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">𝒛</mi><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></msub><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">,</mo><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}=g_{\theta}(\bm{z}_{0},\beta=0)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A4.SS4.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. We train our non-linear diffusion models for </span><math id="A4.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1000</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A4.SS4.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> steps (refer to </span><a href="#A3.T6" title="In C.1.3 Unconditional Image Generation ‣ C.1 Details of Implementation ‣ Appendix C Details of experiments ‣ Denoising Multi-𝛽 VAE: Representation Learning for Disentanglement and Generation" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a><span id="A4.SS4.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">) and thus sample for </span><math id="A4.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1000</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A4.SS4.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> steps.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><img src="2507.06613v1/celebahq_4x10.png" id="A4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/201;" width="476" height="201" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><img src="2507.06613v1/ffhq_4x10.png" id="A4.F6.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/201;" width="476" height="201" alt="Refer to caption"></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><img src="2507.06613v1/LSUN_4x10.png" id="A4.F6.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/201;" width="476" height="201" alt="Refer to caption"></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A4.F6.17" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="A4.F6.18" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Samples generated by models trained on CelebA-HQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karras et al., 2018</a>)</cite> (top), FFHQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karras et al., 2019</a>)</cite> (middle), and LSUN-Bedrooms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2015</a>)</cite> (bottom). These samples demonstrate that our model can function as a standalone generative model.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.5 </span>Exploring the Spectrum of Learned Latents</h3>

<figure id="A4.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A4.F7.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2507.06613v1/figures/interp_beta=0.png" id="A4.F7.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/126;" width="476" height="126" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A4.F7.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><math id="A4.F7.sf1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0/1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">β</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">0</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">1000</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0/1000</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A4.F7.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2507.06613v1/figures/interp_beta=300.png" id="A4.F7.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/127;" width="476" height="127" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A4.F7.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><math id="A4.F7.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=300/1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">β</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">300</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">1000</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=300/1000</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A4.F7.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2507.06613v1/figures/interp_beta=600.png" id="A4.F7.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/127;" width="476" height="127" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A4.F7.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><math id="A4.F7.sf3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=600/1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">β</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">600</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">1000</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=600/1000</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A4.F7.sf4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2507.06613v1/figures/interp_beta=900.png" id="A4.F7.sf4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/127;" width="476" height="127" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A4.F7.sf4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(d)</span> </span><math id="A4.F7.sf4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=900/1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">β</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">900</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.900em">1000</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=900/1000</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A4.F7.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>: </span><span id="A4.F7.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Interpolation in latent space:<span id="A4.F7.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
We interpolate pairs of images (shown in the leftmost and rightmost columns) by applying slerp to the latent spaces.
</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We investigate the properties of latent spaces by interpolating pairs of images using spherical linear interpolation (slerp), as shown in </span><a href="#A4.F7" title="In D.5 Exploring the Spectrum of Learned Latents ‣ Appendix D Additional Results ‣ Denoising Multi-𝛽 VAE: Representation Learning for Disentanglement and Generation" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a><span id="A4.SS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. We selected four values for </span><math id="A4.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A4.SS5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> to explore how the </span><math id="A4.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A4.SS5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> value affects the interpolated images. The latent space with the smallest value, </span><math id="A4.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A4.SS5.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, is not regularized by the KL term, resulting in continuous but meaningless interpolation. As we gradually increase the value of </span><math id="A4.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A4.SS5.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, the interpolated images transition more smoothly in the meaningful pixel space. Larger values of </span><math id="A4.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A4.SS5.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> yield more natural-looking face images, although this comes at the cost of continuity in the image transitions. It is worth noting that even with large </span><math id="A4.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A4.SS5.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> values, such as </span><math id="A4.SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=900/1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">900</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">/</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">1000</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=900/1000</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A4.SS5.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">, the resulting images maintain high fidelity, which is a distinct property compared to traditional </span><math id="A4.SS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A4.SS5.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">-VAE. Additionally, we show examples of attribute changes in </span><a href="#A4.F8" title="In D.5 Exploring the Spectrum of Learned Latents ‣ Appendix D Additional Results ‣ Denoising Multi-𝛽 VAE: Representation Learning for Disentanglement and Generation" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Fig.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a><span id="A4.SS5.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">. We follow Algorithm </span><a href="#algorithm4" title="Algorithm 4 ‣ C.2 Latent manipulation and interpolation ‣ Appendix C Details of experiments ‣ Denoising Multi-𝛽 VAE: Representation Learning for Disentanglement and Generation" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a><span id="A4.SS5.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> to obtain the manipulated images.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.F8" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2507.06613v1/editing_big_ex.png" id="A4.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:191/350;" width="191" height="350" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A4.F8.13" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 8</span>: </span><span id="A4.F8.14" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">This figure contains more examples of editing of facial attributes of a person. In the first row we modify hair colour, in the second row we add a smile, in the third row we add sunglasses, in the fourth row we remove spectacles and in the final row we keep the spectacles while adding a smile. We source these input images from the CelebA-HQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karras et al., 2018</a>)</cite> and FFHQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karras et al., 2019</a>)</cite> datasets.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Limitations and Broader Impacts</h2>

<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Limitations.</h5>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Our model is trained in a completely unsupervised manner. While this approach allows us to learn disentangled representations without the need for labeled data, it necessitates the use of additional tools, such as PCA, to edit input images as desired, as demonstrated in the appendix. Although our primary focus is on developing generative models that can learn disentangled representations and perform downstream tasks without relying on extra conditioning, we recognize that incorporating text conditioning capabilities into our method could lead to more interpretable representations. This is a promising direction for future work.
Furthermore, our sampling method for the non-linear diffusion model is similar to DDPM rather than DDIM. Consequently, developing a DDIM-based sampling method for faster generation remains an area for future exploration.
Lastly, non-linear diffusion model is still a relatively unexplored area compared to linear diffusion models. While our model demonstrates promising results, we anticipate that future research focused on non-linear diffusion model could further enhance generation quality and expand the capabilities of generative models.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Broader Impacts.</h5>

<div id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">As with any general image generation model, our approach carries the risk of producing harmful or inappropriate content, which is contingent upon the datasets used for training. We have utilized image datasets that have been widely accepted for academic purposes. However, it is essential to acknowledge that even well-curated datasets may contain biases or reflect societal stereotypes, which can inadvertently influence the outputs of our model.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</article>
</div>
</div>
